---
// KiwiDesk — Learn › Getting started (English).
//
// The first newcomer page. Warm, plain-language, non-technical
// voice (matches the landing's Simple mode). Content is grounded
// in docs/user-guide.md (onboarding wizard, Accessibility
// permission, no SIP) and the landing's build-from-source block.
// Structure is built to be localized later like the landing —
// for now the copy is inlined; when German /learn/ ships it can
// move behind a per-locale strings prop the way Landing.astro does.
import LearnLayout from "../../layouts/LearnLayout.astro";

const repo = "https://github.com/hajiboy95/KiwiDesk";

const title = "Getting started — KiwiDesk";
const description =
  "Getting started with KiwiDesk is quiet and quick. Here's " +
  "what to expect the first time it tidies your windows.";
---

<LearnLayout active="getting-started" title={title}
  description={description}>
  <header class="learn__head">
    <span class="learn__eyebrow">Learn</span>
    <h1 class="learn__title">Getting started</h1>
    <p class="learn__lede">
      There are no setup rituals and no manual to read. Once
      KiwiDesk is running, it quietly starts helping — you open
      windows the way you always have, and they settle into a
      tidy layout on their own. Here's what the first few minutes
      look like.
    </p>
  </header>

  <!-- ============ Install ============ -->
  <section class="learn-section">
    <h2>Installing KiwiDesk</h2>
    <p>
      KiwiDesk is in an early command-line beta right now, so
      today you install it by building it from source. It sounds
      more technical than it is — three lines in the Terminal app
      and you're done. Copy them, paste them, press return.
    </p>

    <div class="code" aria-label="Build KiwiDesk from source">
      <div class="code__bar">
        <span class="code__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="code__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="code__dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span class="code__title">Terminal — build</span>
        <button class="code__copy" id="copyBuild" type="button"
          data-copy={`git clone ${repo}.git\ncd KiwiDesk\nswift build -c release`}
          aria-label="Copy build commands">
          <svg class="code__copy-icon" width="15" height="15"
            viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
            stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"
            stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <rect x="9" y="9" width="12" height="12" rx="2" />
            <path d="M5 15H4a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V4a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h9a2 2 0 0 1 2 2v1" />
          </svg>
          <span class="code__copy-label">Copy</span>
        </button>
      </div>
      <pre><code><span class="prompt">$</span> git clone {repo}.git
<span class="prompt">$</span> cd KiwiDesk
<span class="prompt">$</span> swift build -c release
<span class="cmt"># that's it — the built app lives in .build/release</span></code></pre>
    </div>

    <p class="learn__store-note">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
        stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round"
        stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M12 3 5 6v5c0 4 3 7 7 8 4-1 7-4 7-8V6l-7-3Z" />
      </svg>
      Requires macOS 14 or later. A packaged, one-click app is on
      its way to the Mac App Store — no Terminal needed once it
      lands.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Accessibility permission ============ -->
  <section class="learn-section">
    <h2>The one permission it asks for</h2>
    <p>
      The very first time you open KiwiDesk, macOS asks you to
      grant <strong>Accessibility</strong> access. This is the
      normal, expected prompt — and it's the only thing KiwiDesk
      needs from you.
    </p>
    <p>
      Why? To tidy your screen, KiwiDesk has to be able to move
      and resize your windows for you. macOS keeps that ability
      behind the Accessibility permission, so apps can't rearrange
      your desktop without you saying yes first. That's exactly
      what you're saying yes to here.
    </p>

    <div class="learn-callout learn-callout--trust">
      <span class="learn-callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M12 3 5 6v5c0 4 3 7 7 8 4-1 7-4 7-8V6l-7-3Z" />
          <path d="m9 12 2 2 4-4" />
        </svg>
      </span>
      <div class="learn-callout__body">
        <h3>What it never does</h3>
        <p>
          KiwiDesk stays entirely in normal user space. To be
          clear about the things people worry about:
        </p>
        <ul class="learn-checklist">
          <li>
            <span class="learn-checklist__check" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"
                stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            It never asks you to disable SIP (macOS System
            Integrity Protection). Your Mac's built-in protections
            stay exactly as they are.
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="learn-checklist__check" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none"
                stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="3"
                stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round">
                <path d="M20 6 9 17l-5-5" />
              </svg>
            </span>
            It never needs Input Monitoring. Its keyboard shortcuts
            use a built-in macOS mechanism, so it isn't — and can't
            be — a keylogger.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="learn-callout">
      <span class="learn-callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round">
          <circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9" />
          <path d="M12 16v-4M12 8h.01" />
        </svg>
      </span>
      <div class="learn-callout__body">
        <h3>You won't be left to figure it out</h3>
        <p>
          A short onboarding wizard walks you through granting the
          permission step by step, pointing you to the right spot
          in <strong>System Settings › Privacy &amp; Security ›
          Accessibility</strong>. If you use more than one display,
          it may also suggest turning on "Displays have separate
          Spaces" — optional, but it makes multi-monitor tidying
          work better.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Your first window ============ -->
  <section class="learn-section">
    <h2>Your first tidy window</h2>
    <p>
      With the permission granted, KiwiDesk gets to work. The
      moment you have a window open, you'll see it settle neatly
      into place — filling its share of the screen, edge to edge,
      with no overlap. Open a second window and the two share the
      space side by side. Open a third and they rebalance again,
      each one still fully visible. Nothing is buried, nothing is
      hunted for.
    </p>

    <div class="learn-snap" aria-hidden="true">
      <svg viewBox="0 0 320 150" preserveAspectRatio="xMidYMid meet">
        <!-- Loose window drifting in -->
        <rect x="18" y="24" width="96" height="70" rx="6"
          class="tile tile--ghost" />
        <!-- Arrow -->
        <path d="M132 59h34m0 0-9-9m9 9-9 9" fill="none"
          stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.4"
          stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"
          style="color: var(--text-faint)" />
        <!-- Tidy pair snapped into place -->
        <rect x="188" y="18" width="56" height="112" rx="6"
          class="tile" />
        <rect x="248" y="18" width="56" height="53" rx="6"
          class="tile tile--accent" />
        <rect x="248" y="77" width="56" height="53" rx="6"
          class="tile" />
      </svg>
    </div>

    <p>
      That's the whole idea. You keep working the way you always
      do — open apps, browse, write — and the arranging just
      happens in the background. When you do want to nudge the
      layout, a tap of a keyboard shortcut does it. Otherwise it
      stays out of your way and keeps things neat.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ If something looks off ============ -->
  <section class="learn-section">
    <h2>If something looks off</h2>
    <p>
      Take a breath — nothing here is destructive. KiwiDesk only
      moves and resizes windows; it never touches your files, your
      apps, or your data. If a layout ever isn't to your liking,
      you're always in control.
    </p>

    <div class="learn-callout">
      <span class="learn-callout__icon" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="1.9" stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M12 2v10M8 6a6 6 0 1 0 8 0" />
        </svg>
      </span>
      <div class="learn-callout__body">
        <h3>You can quit anytime</h3>
        <p>
          Quit KiwiDesk from its menu-bar icon whenever you like.
          The moment it's off, your windows behave exactly like
          normal macOS windows again — free to drag, resize, and
          overlap however you please. Nothing sticks. You can
          always start it back up when you want the calm again.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ============ Next card ============ -->
  <a class="learn-next" href="/learn/layouts/">
    <span class="learn-next__label">Next</span>
    <div class="learn-next__row">
      <div>
        <h2 class="learn-next__title">Meet the layouts</h2>
        <p class="learn-next__sub">
          Curious what just happened to your windows? See the
          handful of tidy arrangements KiwiDesk can use — and how
          they each keep your screen calm.
        </p>
      </div>
      <span class="learn-next__arrow" aria-hidden="true">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor"
          stroke-width="2.4" stroke-linecap="round"
          stroke-linejoin="round">
          <path d="M5 12h14M13 6l6 6-6 6" />
        </svg>
      </span>
    </div>
  </a>

  <script>
    // ---- Copy the build commands ----
    const copyBtn = document.getElementById("copyBuild");
    const copyLabel = copyBtn?.querySelector(".code__copy-label");
    copyBtn?.addEventListener("click", async () => {
      const text = copyBtn.getAttribute("data-copy") || "";
      try {
        await navigator.clipboard.writeText(text);
        copyBtn.classList.add("is-copied");
        if (copyLabel) copyLabel.textContent = "Copied";
        setTimeout(() => {
          copyBtn.classList.remove("is-copied");
          if (copyLabel) copyLabel.textContent = "Copy";
        }, 1600);
      } catch (e) {
        /* clipboard blocked — no-op */
      }
    });
  </script>
</LearnLayout>
